Charles “Charlie” Hall, the Bainbridge Island resident who invented the modern waterbed in 1968, is preparing a redesigned “Waterbed 2018” and plans a test-market launch this spring or summer. Hall, now 71 and a successful designer with about 40 patents (including the Sun Shower), originally developed the waterbed as a graduate-student project in San Francisco; it became an iconic product of the 1970s counterculture and reached peak popularity in the 1980s. 

The new version keeps the core idea—conforming, warm support resembling floating—while addressing the old model’s drawbacks. Improvements include:
- a foam collar instead of a rigid wooden frame for easier entry/exit;
- spandex top covers for a floating sensation;
- fiber inserts and new materials to suppress wave action;
- updated dual-bladder temperature control so each side can be adjusted;
- a potential bed-in-a-box direct-to-consumer offering.

Hall expects queen and king sizes; a complete system will retail around $2,000. The initial retail partner will be a Florida furniture chain owned by a longtime friend; broader rollout may follow. Hall notes many misconceptions hurt the product’s reputation—movies and cheap knockoffs emphasized leaks and sloshing—plus price wars and poor marketing in later years. He also points out waterbeds influenced the mattress industry overall (memory foam, pillowtops). While he doesn’t need the money, Hall remains an active tinkerer and believes the waterbed could appeal both to nostalgic buyers and to millennials unfamiliar with the format.

A group of 44 tech and clean‑transportation leaders, including Tesla CEO Elon Musk, urged the California Air Resources Board (CARB) to stop requiring Volkswagen to repair its emissions‑cheating diesel cars and instead compel VW to accelerate its transition to zero‑emission vehicles (ZEVs). Their argument: modern diesel technology has reached physical and economic limits—making it extremely costly or impossible to meet tighter standards without sacrificing performance—so many affected cars cannot be practically or acceptably fixed, and retrofits (like urea systems) are impractical for small cars and can raise CO2.

Their proposal, to be codified in an enforceable agreement, calls for CARB to:
- Release VW from obligations to fix noncompliant diesel cars in California;
- Require VW to greatly accelerate its rollout of ZEVs, achieving at least a 10:1 reduction in pollutant emissions compared with the diesel cheating fleet within five years;
- Have VW invest the amounts it would otherwise have paid in fines into U.S. manufacturing and R&D (particularly in California) to build ZEVs and battery plants;
- Allow VW flexibility in implementation via ZEV credits.

The signatories argue this approach would yield greater emissions reductions, create California jobs, avoid wasting money on infeasible fixes, and speed the global shift to emissions‑free transportation—while sidestepping future cheating opportunities inherent to combustion vehicles.
